Context and authorship. This folio is not a Riemann derivation. It continues R. Dedekind's handwritten draft of the 'Erläuterungen zu den vorstehenden Fragmenten' (Riemann, Werke, 1876; commentary on Riemann's two fragments on limit values of elliptic modular functions). The previous page begins with Dedekind's title and his name. The page is the setup for what became Dedekind's theory of log eta and the 'Dedekind sums'.

Notation: 1^z := e^{2πiz}, ω = x+iy with y>0, q = 1^{ω/2} = e^{πiω}.
η(ω) = 1^{ω/24} ∏_{ν≥1}(1−1^{ων}) = q^{1/12}∏(1−q^{2ν}). This is the modern Dedekind eta η(τ) with τ=ω.

(1) Single-valuedness. η has no zeros or poles in the simply connected upper half-plane. Hence log η and all powers η^a are single-valued once fixed at one point. Normalization: log η(ω) − πiω/12 → 0 as y→∞, i.e. q→0. Equivalently, log η = πiω/12 + Σ log(1−q^{2ν}) with principal branches near q=0.

(2) Quoted identities (Jacobi, Fundamenta nova §36), with 1^{1/48}=e^{πi/24}, 1^{-1/24}=e^{-πi/12}:
  η(2ω) η(ω/2) η((1+ω)/2) = e^{πi/24} η(ω)^3;
  k^{1/4} = e^{πi/24} √2 η(2ω)/η((1+ω)/2);
  k'^{1/4} = e^{πi/24} η(ω/2)/η((1+ω)/2);
  (2K/π)^{1/2} = e^{-πi/12} η((1+ω)/2)^2/η(ω).

(3) Logarithmic form (I). Take logs with the q→0 normalizations. These are fixed (as in Fund. nova §40) by requiring that log k − log 4 − πiω/2 = log(k/(4√q)), log k' and log(2K/π) all tend to 0 as q→0:
  log η(2ω) + log η(ω/2) + log η((1+ω)/2) = πi/24 + 3 log η(ω);
  log k = log 4 + πi/6 + 4 log η(2ω) − 4 log η((1+ω)/2);
  log k' = πi/6 + 4 log η(ω/2) − 4 log η((1+ω)/2);
  log(2K/π) = −πi/6 + 4 log η((1+ω)/2) − 2 log η(ω).
The constants are fixed by comparing leading terms (my verification). For example, log η((1+ω)/2) ~ πi/24 + πiω/24 and log η(2ω) ~ πiω/6. Hence the RHS of log k ~ log 4 + πiω/2, as required.

(4) Programme. Combine this q→0 behaviour with the first-order (unimodular) transformation of theta functions. This yields the behaviour as ω tends to a rational real number, i.e. q tends to a root of unity. Irrational real points are said not yet to belong to the boundary 'in a certain sense'.

(5) Theta function:
  ϑ1(z,ω) = Σ_s exp 2πi[(s+½)^2 ω/2 + (s+½)(z−½)]
          = 2 η(ω) 1^{ω/12} sin(πz) ∏_{ν≥1}(1−q^{2ν}e^{2πiz})(1−q^{2ν}e^{−2πiz}).
This is the Jacobi triple product. Note that η(ω)1^{ω/12} = q^{1/4}∏(1−q^{2ν}). The next page then derives ϑ1'(0) = 2πη^3 and the transformation law of log η with an integer k, which is Dedekind's sum.
